<header><p class="st-kicker">electorate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Electorate of Hanover</h1><p class="st-meta">1692 CE – 1814 CE · capital Hanover</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Brunswick-Lüneburg electorate whose rulers became kings of Great Britain, linking a north German state to a global empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-elector-of-hanover">Ernest Augustus</a> of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, was granted a ninth electoral dignity by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Leopold</a> I in 1692 in return for military support, though it was recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> only in 1708. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-of-hanover">Sophia</a> was named heir to the British throne by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> of 1701.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1714 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-of-hanover">Sophia</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-great-britain">George Louis</a> became King George I of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> was governed by a privy council while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> lived mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The state was Lutheran, agrarian and conservative, run by a nobility-dominated administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-of-great-britain">George II</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> in 1734, which became a leading centre of learning. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrenhausen-gardens">Herrenhausen</a> gardens survive from Sophia's time.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in the Napoleonic wars and partly absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a>. In 1814 the restored state was raised to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/electorate-of-hanover.jpg" alt="Electorate of Hanover" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Oren neu dag · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hanover_(1692).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1692 CE – 1814 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>electorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hanover</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernest Augustus, George I, George III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Electorate of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Kurhannover</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1753&amp;focus=electorate-of-hanover" aria-label="Electorate of Hanover on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4899 370 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Electorate of Hanover lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5219 626L4997 651M5219 626L5239 589M5219 626L5232 601M5219 626L5219 626M5219 626L5229 694M5219 626L5215 656M5219 626L5219 626M5219 626L5161 628M5219 626L5211 592M5219 626L5183 629"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5239 589h0M5232 601h0M5219 626h0M5229 694h0M5215 656h0M5219 626h0M516